  "include": "directive:DIRECTIVE_051",
  "context": "Directive DIRECTIVE_051: Supply-Chain Install Safety\n    Intent: Install-time and dependency-resolution decisions must be evaluated against a threat-CLASS model, not a static package denylist. Worm-style supply-chain compromises (credential-harvesting postinstall scripts, typosquatted or namespace-confused packages, rapid-republish attacks during an active incident window) recur across ecosystems faster than any hand-maintained list of bad package/version pairs can track. Agents and mission workflows must therefore default to five threat-class controls for every dependency addition or upgrade: (1) official registry authenticity \u2014 the resolved package and version must come from the approved official registry, not an unexpected mirror or namespace; (2) package freshness visibility \u2014 first-publish and latest-publish/update timestamps must be surfaced so an operator can see when a version is suspiciously new or was substantially reworked; (3) deny-by-default lifecycle-script approval \u2014 `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall` scripts are never auto-approved and must be explicitly allowlisted with a documented justification when required for legitimate native builds; (4) Node Active LTS awareness \u2014 the project/runtime Node version is checked against current Active LTS and any skew is explicitly disclosed with rationale, never silently accepted; (5) incident-list and IoC/persistence posture \u2014 known active-incident package/version lists and indicator-of-compromise (IoC) or persistence patterns (unexpected postinstall network calls, credential/token exfiltration attempts, self-propagating install scripts) are treated as class-level risk signals to check for, with CAS/vendor incident feeds as the external authority of record \u2014 this directive never hardcodes specific incident package names or versions as core truth, because that list changes faster than doctrine can be re-authored.\n    Scope: Applies whenever a software-dev mission adds, upgrades, or reviews a dependency during `plan`, `implement`, or `review`, across any package ecosystem (npm/yarn/pnpm for JavaScript/TypeScript, pip/uv for Python, Maven/Gradle for Java, and others as applicable). It governs the checks an implementer must perform before accepting a new or changed dependency and the evidence a reviewer must require before approving a change that touches the dependency tree. Per NFR-003/C-003 of the supply-chain-security-checks-layer mission, this directive is advisory at the transition-gate level in v1: it does not introduce a new fail-closed transition-gate handler, and workflow wiring must not silently hard-block on it. It DOES require visibility and explicit evidence \u2014 silence or an unexamined default is not compliance.\n    Procedures:\n      - Registry authenticity check: before accepting a new or changed dependency, confirm the package and version resolve on the approved official registry (e.g. registry.npmjs.org for npm, PyPI for pip/uv, Maven Central for Java) and not an unexpected mirror, proxy, or typosquatted namespace. If resolution is ambiguous or points somewhere unexpected, stop and require explicit operator attention rather than proceeding silently.\n      - Package freshness signal: surface first-publish and latest-publish/update timestamps for the resolved version as part of the dependency decision. A package or version published very recently, especially during a known active incident window, is a risk signal that must be visible to the operator and acknowledged, not silently accepted.\n      - Lifecycle script approval discipline: treat `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall` (and ecosystem equivalents) as deny-by-default. Never enable automatic execution of lifecycle scripts for a new or changed dependency. When a legitimate native build genuinely requires a lifecycle script, require an explicit, documented allowlist entry with the justification recorded \u2014 blanket script enablement is never an acceptable substitute for a scoped allowlist.\n      - Node Active LTS awareness and skew disclosure: check the project's declared/runtime Node version against the current Active LTS line. If the project runs a non-LTS or outdated Node version, that is elevated risk that must be explicitly disclosed with a documented business rationale \u2014 it may be acceptable, but never silently.\n      - Incident-list and IoC/persistence posture: check newly added or upgraded packages against current CAS/vendor supply-chain incident feeds (the external authority of record for active incidents) and watch for class-level indicators of compromise \u2014 unexpected network calls from install scripts, credential or token exfiltration attempts, or self-propagating (worm-style) install behavior. This directive encodes the threat classes to check for; it does not embed a static list of incident package names/versions as core doctrine, per C-004 (no live external denylist sync in core).\n      - Record evidence, not just a pass/fail impression: implementer and reviewer outputs must capture what was checked (registry, freshness, scripts, Node LTS, incident/IoC posture) and the resulting decision, so a later reviewer or operator does not have to re-derive the analysis.\n    Integrity rules:\n      - A dependency resolving to an unexpected registry, mirror, or namespace must never be silently accepted; it requires explicit operator attention before the change proceeds.\n      - Lifecycle scripts are never auto-approved. A lifecycle script may only run when explicitly allowlisted with a recorded justification \u2014 blanket enablement (e.g. globally disabling script safety) is a violation of this directive, not a valid workaround.\n      - Node LTS skew must never be silently accepted; a non-LTS or outdated Node baseline requires an explicit, documented rationale even when the project has legitimate business reasons to run it.\n      - This directive must remain threat-class based. Hardcoding specific incident package names or version numbers as core doctrine truth is prohibited \u2014 cite CAS/vendor incident lists as the external authority instead, per C-004.\n      - This directive does not introduce a new fail-closed transition-gate handler in v1 (C-003/NFR-003). Workflow wiring that consumes this directive must keep it advisory at the gate level while still requiring explicit visibility and evidence \u2014 omitting the check entirely is not an acceptable interpretation of 'advisory'.\n    Validation criteria:\n      - Software-dev mission guidance for plan/implement/review references this directive (directly or via the supply-chain-install-safety tactic) for any change that adds, upgrades, or removes a dependency.\n      - Implementer and reviewer evidence for a dependency change documents registry authenticity, freshness, lifecycle-script disposition, Node LTS posture, and incident/IoC posture \u2014 an unexamined default is treated as a gap, not a pass.\n      - No dependency change is accepted with an unreviewed non-default lifecycle script or an unacknowledged Node LTS skew.\n      - No hardcoded incident-specific package name/version list is introduced into this directive as core truth; CAS/vendor incident feeds remain the external authority referenced.\n    Full artifact:\n      {\n        \"enforcement\": \"required\",\n        \"explicit_allowances\": [],\n        \"id\": \"DIRECTIVE_051\",\n        \"integrity_rules\": [\n          \"A dependency resolving to an unexpected registry, mirror, or namespace must never be silently accepted; it requires explicit operator attention before the change proceeds.\",\n          \"Lifecycle scripts are never auto-approved. A lifecycle script may only run when explicitly allowlisted with a recorded justification \\u2014 blanket enablement (e.g. globally disabling script safety) is a violation of this directive, not a valid workaround.\",\n          \"Node LTS skew must never be silently accepted; a non-LTS or outdated Node baseline requires an explicit, documented rationale even when the project has legitimate business reasons to run it.\",\n          \"This directive must remain threat-class based. Hardcoding specific incident package names or version numbers as core doctrine truth is prohibited \\u2014 cite CAS/vendor incident lists as the external authority instead, per C-004.\",\n          \"This directive does not introduce a new fail-closed transition-gate handler in v1 (C-003/NFR-003). Workflow wiring that consumes this directive must keep it advisory at the gate level while still requiring explicit visibility and evidence \\u2014 omitting the check entirely is not an acceptable interpretation of 'advisory'.\"\n        ],\n        \"intent\": \"Install-time and dependency-resolution decisions must be evaluated against a threat-CLASS model, not a static package denylist. Worm-style supply-chain compromises (credential-harvesting postinstall scripts, typosquatted or namespace-confused packages, rapid-republish attacks during an active incident window) recur across ecosystems faster than any hand-maintained list of bad package/version pairs can track. Agents and mission workflows must therefore default to five threat-class controls for every dependency addition or upgrade: (1) official registry authenticity \\u2014 the resolved package and version must come from the approved official registry, not an unexpected mirror or namespace; (2) package freshness visibility \\u2014 first-publish and latest-publish/update timestamps must be surfaced so an operator can see when a version is suspiciously new or was substantially reworked; (3) deny-by-default lifecycle-script approval \\u2014 `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall` scripts are never auto-approved and must be explicitly allowlisted with a documented justification when required for legitimate native builds; (4) Node Active LTS awareness \\u2014 the project/runtime Node version is checked against current Active LTS and any skew is explicitly disclosed with rationale, never silently accepted; (5) incident-list and IoC/persistence posture \\u2014 known active-incident package/version lists and indicator-of-compromise (IoC) or persistence patterns (unexpected postinstall network calls, credential/token exfiltration attempts, self-propagating install scripts) are treated as class-level risk signals to check for, with CAS/vendor incident feeds as the external authority of record \\u2014 this directive never hardcodes specific incident package names or versions as core truth, because that list changes faster than doctrine can be re-authored.\\n\",\n        \"procedures\": [\n          \"Registry authenticity check: before accepting a new or changed dependency, confirm the package and version resolve on the approved official registry (e.g. registry.npmjs.org for npm, PyPI for pip/uv, Maven Central for Java) and not an unexpected mirror, proxy, or typosquatted namespace. If resolution is ambiguous or points somewhere unexpected, stop and require explicit operator attention rather than proceeding silently.\",\n          \"Package freshness signal: surface first-publish and latest-publish/update timestamps for the resolved version as part of the dependency decision. A package or version published very recently, especially during a known active incident window, is a risk signal that must be visible to the operator and acknowledged, not silently accepted.\",\n          \"Lifecycle script approval discipline: treat `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall` (and ecosystem equivalents) as deny-by-default. Never enable automatic execution of lifecycle scripts for a new or changed dependency. When a legitimate native build genuinely requires a lifecycle script, require an explicit, documented allowlist entry with the justification recorded \\u2014 blanket script enablement is never an acceptable substitute for a scoped allowlist.\",\n          \"Node Active LTS awareness and skew disclosure: check the project's declared/runtime Node version against the current Active LTS line. If the project runs a non-LTS or outdated Node version, that is elevated risk that must be explicitly disclosed with a documented business rationale \\u2014 it may be acceptable, but never silently.\",\n          \"Incident-list and IoC/persistence posture: check newly added or upgraded packages against current CAS/vendor supply-chain incident feeds (the external authority of record for active incidents) and watch for class-level indicators of compromise \\u2014 unexpected network calls from install scripts, credential or token exfiltration attempts, or self-propagating (worm-style) install behavior. This directive encodes the threat classes to check for; it does not embed a static list of incident package names/versions as core doctrine, per C-004 (no live external denylist sync in core).\",\n          \"Record evidence, not just a pass/fail impression: implementer and reviewer outputs must capture what was checked (registry, freshness, scripts, Node LTS, incident/IoC posture) and the resulting decision, so a later reviewer or operator does not have to re-derive the analysis.\"\n        ],\n        \"references\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"DIRECTIVE_001\",\n            \"type\": \"directive\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"dependency-hygiene\",\n            \"type\": \"tactic\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"supply-chain-install-safety\",\n            \"type\": \"tactic\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"schema_version\": \"1.0\",\n        \"scope\": \"Applies whenever a software-dev mission adds, upgrades, or reviews a dependency during `plan`, `implement`, or `review`, across any package ecosystem (npm/yarn/pnpm for JavaScript/TypeScript, pip/uv for Python, Maven/Gradle for Java, and others as applicable). It governs the checks an implementer must perform before accepting a new or changed dependency and the evidence a reviewer must require before approving a change that touches the dependency tree. Per NFR-003/C-003 of the supply-chain-security-checks-layer mission, this directive is advisory at the transition-gate level in v1: it does not introduce a new fail-closed transition-gate handler, and workflow wiring must not silently hard-block on it. It DOES require visibility and explicit evidence \\u2014 silence or an unexamined default is not compliance.\\n\",\n        \"title\": \"Supply-Chain Install Safety\",\n        \"validation_criteria\": [\n          \"Software-dev mission guidance for plan/implement/review references this directive (directly or via the supply-chain-install-safety tactic) for any change that adds, upgrades, or removes a dependency.\",\n          \"Implementer and reviewer evidence for a dependency change documents registry authenticity, freshness, lifecycle-script disposition, Node LTS posture, and incident/IoC posture \\u2014 an unexamined default is treated as a gap, not a pass.\",\n          \"No dependency change is accepted with an unreviewed non-default lifecycle script or an unacknowledged Node LTS skew.\",\n          \"No hardcoded incident-specific package name/version list is introduced into this directive as core truth; CAS/vendor incident feeds remain the external authority referenced.\"\n        ]\n      }",
  "text": "Directive DIRECTIVE_051: Supply-Chain Install Safety\n    Intent: Install-time and dependency-resolution decisions must be evaluated against a threat-CLASS model, not a static package denylist. Worm-style supply-chain compromises (credential-harvesting postinstall scripts, typosquatted or namespace-confused packages, rapid-republish attacks during an active incident window) recur across ecosystems faster than any hand-maintained list of bad package/version pairs can track. Agents and mission workflows must therefore default to five threat-class controls for every dependency addition or upgrade: (1) official registry authenticity \u2014 the resolved package and version must come from the approved official registry, not an unexpected mirror or namespace; (2) package freshness visibility \u2014 first-publish and latest-publish/update timestamps must be surfaced so an operator can see when a version is suspiciously new or was substantially reworked; (3) deny-by-default lifecycle-script approval \u2014 `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall` scripts are never auto-approved and must be explicitly allowlisted with a documented justification when required for legitimate native builds; (4) Node Active LTS awareness \u2014 the project/runtime Node version is checked against current Active LTS and any skew is explicitly disclosed with rationale, never silently accepted; (5) incident-list and IoC/persistence posture \u2014 known active-incident package/version lists and indicator-of-compromise (IoC) or persistence patterns (unexpected postinstall network calls, credential/token exfiltration attempts, self-propagating install scripts) are treated as class-level risk signals to check for, with CAS/vendor incident feeds as the external authority of record \u2014 this directive never hardcodes specific incident package names or versions as core truth, because that list changes faster than doctrine can be re-authored.\n    Scope: Applies whenever a software-dev mission adds, upgrades, or reviews a dependency during `plan`, `implement`, or `review`, across any package ecosystem (npm/yarn/pnpm for JavaScript/TypeScript, pip/uv for Python, Maven/Gradle for Java, and others as applicable). It governs the checks an implementer must perform before accepting a new or changed dependency and the evidence a reviewer must require before approving a change that touches the dependency tree. Per NFR-003/C-003 of the supply-chain-security-checks-layer mission, this directive is advisory at the transition-gate level in v1: it does not introduce a new fail-closed transition-gate handler, and workflow wiring must not silently hard-block on it. It DOES require visibility and explicit evidence \u2014 silence or an unexamined default is not compliance.\n    Procedures:\n      - Registry authenticity check: before accepting a new or changed dependency, confirm the package and version resolve on the approved official registry (e.g. registry.npmjs.org for npm, PyPI for pip/uv, Maven Central for Java) and not an unexpected mirror, proxy, or typosquatted namespace. If resolution is ambiguous or points somewhere unexpected, stop and require explicit operator attention rather than proceeding silently.\n      - Package freshness signal: surface first-publish and latest-publish/update timestamps for the resolved version as part of the dependency decision. A package or version published very recently, especially during a known active incident window, is a risk signal that must be visible to the operator and acknowledged, not silently accepted.\n      - Lifecycle script approval discipline: treat `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall` (and ecosystem equivalents) as deny-by-default. Never enable automatic execution of lifecycle scripts for a new or changed dependency. When a legitimate native build genuinely requires a lifecycle script, require an explicit, documented allowlist entry with the justification recorded \u2014 blanket script enablement is never an acceptable substitute for a scoped allowlist.\n      - Node Active LTS awareness and skew disclosure: check the project's declared/runtime Node version against the current Active LTS line. If the project runs a non-LTS or outdated Node version, that is elevated risk that must be explicitly disclosed with a documented business rationale \u2014 it may be acceptable, but never silently.\n      - Incident-list and IoC/persistence posture: check newly added or upgraded packages against current CAS/vendor supply-chain incident feeds (the external authority of record for active incidents) and watch for class-level indicators of compromise \u2014 unexpected network calls from install scripts, credential or token exfiltration attempts, or self-propagating (worm-style) install behavior. This directive encodes the threat classes to check for; it does not embed a static list of incident package names/versions as core doctrine, per C-004 (no live external denylist sync in core).\n      - Record evidence, not just a pass/fail impression: implementer and reviewer outputs must capture what was checked (registry, freshness, scripts, Node LTS, incident/IoC posture) and the resulting decision, so a later reviewer or operator does not have to re-derive the analysis.\n    Integrity rules:\n      - A dependency resolving to an unexpected registry, mirror, or namespace must never be silently accepted; it requires explicit operator attention before the change proceeds.\n      - Lifecycle scripts are never auto-approved. A lifecycle script may only run when explicitly allowlisted with a recorded justification \u2014 blanket enablement (e.g. globally disabling script safety) is a violation of this directive, not a valid workaround.\n      - Node LTS skew must never be silently accepted; a non-LTS or outdated Node baseline requires an explicit, documented rationale even when the project has legitimate business reasons to run it.\n      - This directive must remain threat-class based. Hardcoding specific incident package names or version numbers as core doctrine truth is prohibited \u2014 cite CAS/vendor incident lists as the external authority instead, per C-004.\n      - This directive does not introduce a new fail-closed transition-gate handler in v1 (C-003/NFR-003). Workflow wiring that consumes this directive must keep it advisory at the gate level while still requiring explicit visibility and evidence \u2014 omitting the check entirely is not an acceptable interpretation of 'advisory'.\n    Validation criteria:\n      - Software-dev mission guidance for plan/implement/review references this directive (directly or via the supply-chain-install-safety tactic) for any change that adds, upgrades, or removes a dependency.\n      - Implementer and reviewer evidence for a dependency change documents registry authenticity, freshness, lifecycle-script disposition, Node LTS posture, and incident/IoC posture \u2014 an unexamined default is treated as a gap, not a pass.\n      - No dependency change is accepted with an unreviewed non-default lifecycle script or an unacknowledged Node LTS skew.\n      - No hardcoded incident-specific package name/version list is introduced into this directive as core truth; CAS/vendor incident feeds remain the external authority referenced.\n    Full artifact:\n      {\n        \"enforcement\": \"required\",\n        \"explicit_allowances\": [],\n        \"id\": \"DIRECTIVE_051\",\n        \"integrity_rules\": [\n          \"A dependency resolving to an unexpected registry, mirror, or namespace must never be silently accepted; it requires explicit operator attention before the change proceeds.\",\n          \"Lifecycle scripts are never auto-approved. A lifecycle script may only run when explicitly allowlisted with a recorded justification \\u2014 blanket enablement (e.g. globally disabling script safety) is a violation of this directive, not a valid workaround.\",\n          \"Node LTS skew must never be silently accepted; a non-LTS or outdated Node baseline requires an explicit, documented rationale even when the project has legitimate business reasons to run it.\",\n          \"This directive must remain threat-class based. Hardcoding specific incident package names or version numbers as core doctrine truth is prohibited \\u2014 cite CAS/vendor incident lists as the external authority instead, per C-004.\",\n          \"This directive does not introduce a new fail-closed transition-gate handler in v1 (C-003/NFR-003). Workflow wiring that consumes this directive must keep it advisory at the gate level while still requiring explicit visibility and evidence \\u2014 omitting the check entirely is not an acceptable interpretation of 'advisory'.\"\n        ],\n        \"intent\": \"Install-time and dependency-resolution decisions must be evaluated against a threat-CLASS model, not a static package denylist. Worm-style supply-chain compromises (credential-harvesting postinstall scripts, typosquatted or namespace-confused packages, rapid-republish attacks during an active incident window) recur across ecosystems faster than any hand-maintained list of bad package/version pairs can track. Agents and mission workflows must therefore default to five threat-class controls for every dependency addition or upgrade: (1) official registry authenticity \\u2014 the resolved package and version must come from the approved official registry, not an unexpected mirror or namespace; (2) package freshness visibility \\u2014 first-publish and latest-publish/update timestamps must be surfaced so an operator can see when a version is suspiciously new or was substantially reworked; (3) deny-by-default lifecycle-script approval \\u2014 `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall` scripts are never auto-approved and must be explicitly allowlisted with a documented justification when required for legitimate native builds; (4) Node Active LTS awareness \\u2014 the project/runtime Node version is checked against current Active LTS and any skew is explicitly disclosed with rationale, never silently accepted; (5) incident-list and IoC/persistence posture \\u2014 known active-incident package/version lists and indicator-of-compromise (IoC) or persistence patterns (unexpected postinstall network calls, credential/token exfiltration attempts, self-propagating install scripts) are treated as class-level risk signals to check for, with CAS/vendor incident feeds as the external authority of record \\u2014 this directive never hardcodes specific incident package names or versions as core truth, because that list changes faster than doctrine can be re-authored.\\n\",\n        \"procedures\": [\n          \"Registry authenticity check: before accepting a new or changed dependency, confirm the package and version resolve on the approved official registry (e.g. registry.npmjs.org for npm, PyPI for pip/uv, Maven Central for Java) and not an unexpected mirror, proxy, or typosquatted namespace. If resolution is ambiguous or points somewhere unexpected, stop and require explicit operator attention rather than proceeding silently.\",\n          \"Package freshness signal: surface first-publish and latest-publish/update timestamps for the resolved version as part of the dependency decision. A package or version published very recently, especially during a known active incident window, is a risk signal that must be visible to the operator and acknowledged, not silently accepted.\",\n          \"Lifecycle script approval discipline: treat `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall` (and ecosystem equivalents) as deny-by-default. Never enable automatic execution of lifecycle scripts for a new or changed dependency. When a legitimate native build genuinely requires a lifecycle script, require an explicit, documented allowlist entry with the justification recorded \\u2014 blanket script enablement is never an acceptable substitute for a scoped allowlist.\",\n          \"Node Active LTS awareness and skew disclosure: check the project's declared/runtime Node version against the current Active LTS line. If the project runs a non-LTS or outdated Node version, that is elevated risk that must be explicitly disclosed with a documented business rationale \\u2014 it may be acceptable, but never silently.\",\n          \"Incident-list and IoC/persistence posture: check newly added or upgraded packages against current CAS/vendor supply-chain incident feeds (the external authority of record for active incidents) and watch for class-level indicators of compromise \\u2014 unexpected network calls from install scripts, credential or token exfiltration attempts, or self-propagating (worm-style) install behavior. This directive encodes the threat classes to check for; it does not embed a static list of incident package names/versions as core doctrine, per C-004 (no live external denylist sync in core).\",\n          \"Record evidence, not just a pass/fail impression: implementer and reviewer outputs must capture what was checked (registry, freshness, scripts, Node LTS, incident/IoC posture) and the resulting decision, so a later reviewer or operator does not have to re-derive the analysis.\"\n        ],\n        \"references\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"DIRECTIVE_001\",\n            \"type\": \"directive\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"dependency-hygiene\",\n            \"type\": \"tactic\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"supply-chain-install-safety\",\n            \"type\": \"tactic\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"schema_version\": \"1.0\",\n        \"scope\": \"Applies whenever a software-dev mission adds, upgrades, or reviews a dependency during `plan`, `implement`, or `review`, across any package ecosystem (npm/yarn/pnpm for JavaScript/TypeScript, pip/uv for Python, Maven/Gradle for Java, and others as applicable). It governs the checks an implementer must perform before accepting a new or changed dependency and the evidence a reviewer must require before approving a change that touches the dependency tree. Per NFR-003/C-003 of the supply-chain-security-checks-layer mission, this directive is advisory at the transition-gate level in v1: it does not introduce a new fail-closed transition-gate handler, and workflow wiring must not silently hard-block on it. It DOES require visibility and explicit evidence \\u2014 silence or an unexamined default is not compliance.\\n\",\n        \"title\": \"Supply-Chain Install Safety\",\n        \"validation_criteria\": [\n          \"Software-dev mission guidance for plan/implement/review references this directive (directly or via the supply-chain-install-safety tactic) for any change that adds, upgrades, or removes a dependency.\",\n          \"Implementer and reviewer evidence for a dependency change documents registry authenticity, freshness, lifecycle-script disposition, Node LTS posture, and incident/IoC posture \\u2014 an unexamined default is treated as a gap, not a pass.\",\n          \"No dependency change is accepted with an unreviewed non-default lifecycle script or an unacknowledged Node LTS skew.\",\n          \"No hardcoded incident-specific package name/version list is introduced into this directive as core truth; CAS/vendor incident feeds remain the external authority referenced.\"\n        ]\n      }"
